  "description": "On broadcasting empiricism",
  "body_html": "<p><em>Thanks to&nbsp;<a href=\"https://unseriously-serious.com/\">Arina Berezovsky\u200b</a>,&nbsp;for organising, and the&nbsp;<a href=\"https://www.independentscience.org/\">Independent Science Society</a> for coming. If you do science at home in the UK and want to come, I'd love to here from you via Substack DM.</em></p><p>The <a href=\"https://www.independentscience.org/\">Independent Science Society</a> had a dinner last night. As part of figuring out how to do excellent science ourselves, we discussed the philosophy of writing science well, which Arina and I motivated in <a href=\"https://chillphysicsenjoyer.substack.com/p/how-do-we-write-science\">this essay</a>.</p><p>We discuss the philosophy of writing because the world is confusing. We have LLMs, more science, more text, and more questions about what counts as good empiricism. And so how are we meant to use language to deal with these issues? There are also ethical questions about what it means to write science safely, which is something I was personally curious about going into the dinner.</p><p>To structure the dinner, we printed a list of question prompts along with the essay before hand, and then placed columns of text at each person's table. And then Arina moderated the discussion in a 'single thread format' where only one person spoke at a time, after some initial prompt. Each person got a notepad to take notes. And credit to Arina, people found that this format worked very well. We also had Chatham house rules so people could speak freely without being attributed to their comments.</p><p>Here are some pictures of the event below. A full write up to follow!</p><div class=\"captioned-image-container\"><figure><a class=\"image-link image2 is-viewable-img\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!pmpG!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F9b4e51ec-52a4-4eb5-b0ba-a0281ad214ff_2048x1152.jpe